    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, it’s good to meet you. What brings you in today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S011",
      "patient_response": "Hi, yes, nice to meet you too. Well... what brought me in is that I just keep getting up in meetings, or at dinner, when everyone else is perfectly fine just staying seated. Like, I’ll suddenly feel the need to refill my coffee, or check a handout, or just walk to the back of the room even though nothing is really urgent at all. \n\nAnd speaking of handouts, the new curriculum they just gave us for the 10th-grade history classes is an absolute nightmare. I mean, they expect us to cover the entire Industrial Revolution in two weeks! And the textbook they gave the kids is just... well, it’s practically falling apart. My husband says I shouldn't stress about it so much, but you know how it is when you've been teaching for 30-some years. You get attached to doing things right. We were actually having dinner at that new Italian place downtown last week to celebrate the end of the term—oh, what's it called, Giovanni's? The garlic bread there is incredible, you really have to try it. But anyway, I just couldn't sit still through the appetizers. I kept getting up to look at the dessert case or check on the coats. \n\nWhat was I saying? Oh, right, the meetings. Yes, it's just frustrating because I can't seem to stay in my chair.",
