      "turn": 6,
      "doctor_question": "Did you have trouble sitting still as a child?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "As a child? Oh, absolutely. Even as a little girl, my report cards always said the exact same thing—that I was a very bright student, but I just could not stay in my seat or wait my turn to speak. My mother used to joke that I wore a permanent path in the floorboards between my desk and the pencil sharpener before I was even ten years old. \n\nSpeaking of pencil sharpeners, you know, the old metal ones that were screwed into the wall right by the chalkboard? The ones you really had to crank? Goodness, I miss the smell of the cedar shavings from those. Kids today don't even use wooden pencils anymore. It's all mechanical, or they're just typing away on their Chromebooks. I swear, the penmanship of my current sophomores is an absolute tragedy. Half the time I can't even read their assignments if they do happen to write them out by hand. They don't even teach cursive in most of the elementary schools in our district now, which just breaks my heart. I mean, how are they going to sign their names on a mortgage or a marriage license someday? Just print it in block letters? My third-grade teacher, Mrs. Higgins, would be rolling in her grave. She was an absolute stickler for the Palmer Method. We had to do those loops over and over on that special lined paper... \n\nAnyway, I'm sorry, I'm rambling again. What were we talking about? Oh! Right, yes. I was always up and about as a kid, constantly needing to sharpen my pencil or just moving around the classroom.",
